A||||29||86SE||Whats everyones gas mileage??||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||02-29-2000||10:44 PM||||I have a 1986 V-6.  It has 86000 miles, K&N Air Filter, and I get at very best 20 MPG, usually I am around 18 MPG.  <P>What is everyone elses V-6 MPG??<P>Mine sounds very low, i do get a code 13, for o2 sensor cuircut.  Does the ECM use a default setting when you get a code 13??||||reg||5
Z||000001||ounmongk||02-29-2000||10:55 PM||||I'm still kinda new to the fiero scene, but I think that a bad O2 sensor will make the engine run kinda lean.(your mpg) correct me if I"m wrong though... what do you mean by default setting? AM I missing something here? I think if you get a code 13, you either need to check the connections or replace the sensor. unless you like 18 mpg  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> again, please correct me if I"m wrong, still learning here, folks. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||02-29-2000||11:00 PM||||The ECM controls the fuel:air ratio, and with a bad O2 sensor the ECM dosent get any info from the sensor, so it dosent know how much fuel to put with the air.  So I figured there would have to be some sort of factory set setting to be used if this type of event would happen.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ???||||reg||1
Z||000003||ounmongk||02-29-2000||11:05 PM||||if it had a default setting, then why would there be any need for a code 13? and if it did have a default setting, then I don't think you'd be getting 18 mpg, because everything will be running according to factory specs...  but then again.. I don't know schlock about any of this... ||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||02-29-2000||11:12 PM||||There has to be some "wrong" message sent to the ECM to make me get 18 MPG.  If the o2 sensor is bad the engine wont run 0:0 raitio.  It has to "know" how much fuel to air to give the engine.<P>The ECM is telling me to change the O2 sensor or it will keep giving me 18 MPG.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||02-29-2000||11:24 PM||||My DuckMobile gets about 30 MPG, pretty good but my honda gets closer to 40 mpg. I wonder what kind of mileage my 88GT will get with the 3.4 engine and Isuzu tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000006||olympic||02-29-2000||11:28 PM||||I would think that there would be a default setting that would protect the engine if the 02 sensor was bad.  It would probably run the engine as rich as possible, probably the same setting as when the engine is cold.  Running the engine lean would be very harmful.  I'd spent a couple of bucks and replace the sensor.  You'll probably have to drive the car for a while before the ECM learns a new fuel curve.  Maybe unhook the battery for an hour after installing the new sensor to wipe the ECM memory clean.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||02-29-2000||11:30 PM||||Ever since I talked myself out of the last speeding ticket on a mountain backroad (75 in a 45) my driving has been pretty docile.  I drive 17 miles one way to work on mountain roads mostly at 50 and then 75 on the last 5 miles of interstate (steep mountain pass).  This included the typical traffic in town.  I've been getting 23.8 regularly on my new 2.8L v-6 4spd in my 85SE.<P>On the trip to Daytona my best was 28.7 on interstate 10 between Pensacola and I-75 cruising at 75 with the A/C on.||||reg||6
Z||000008||turbo86se||03-01-2000||12:47 AM||||Gas mileage on my 88 automatic coupe isn't the greatest. I only drive to school and work, and that's not many miles! The increase in gas prices don't help much either. I'm probably getting around 20mpg, it's time to fill it up again, so I'll let everyone know.<P>My V6 86SE, that's another story. Gas mileage is really good on the highway and through town. Once the rpm's get up to about 2200, you hear this hisssssssss, the front end launches up, and the gas gauge goes down!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||b_b_a_d||03-01-2000||02:00 AM||||Hey ya all,<P>Correct me if I'm wrong, but I believe the ECM tries to compensate for any abnormal conditions which may occurr outside of standard operating conditions.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>In regards to an earlier post... <BR>There is no capcitor in the ECM to retain memory settings.  I would say 15 seconds to 1 minute ought to be plenty enough time to purge the system of any current once disconnecting the battery thus resetting the ECM.  On the topic of the ECM learning curve, I have a vague recollection or reading something about normal operating temperatures and about 15 to 30 minutes of highway driving.  I'll look to see if I can find the actual reference for this again.<P>I've been getting around 20 Mpg on my 86 SE 2.8 Automatic with a rebuilt engine and tranny.  Currently have to replace the EGR unit due to a code 32, but I don't think that will give me more than a couple miles more per gallon.      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I'll post the results after replacing the EGR.<P><p>[This message has been edited by b_b_a_d (edited 03-01-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000010||BBBAD GT||03-01-2000||09:36 AM||||I just calculated 26 mph (250 miles from 9.4 gallons) from my newly acquired 87 GT with 85k miles.  I just performed a lil tuneup (cap/rotor/wires/plugs/fuel filter/pcv valve, I think all WAS original before), and it runs great now, fires up on the first turn everytime.  <P>I'm extremely happy.||||reg||1
